+## [C-2] `setOracle` has no access control — anyone can set collateral prices
+
+**Severity**: Critical
+**Category**: evm-audit-access-control, evm-audit-oracles
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:91-94` — `setOracle(PriceOracle)`
+
+**Description**
+`setOracle` is missing `onlyOwner`. The oracle is the sole input to `collateralValueUsd`,
+`debtValueUsd` and therefore `healthFactor` — whoever controls it controls whether any position is
+solvent.
+
+```solidity
+function setOracle(PriceOracle newOracle) external {   // no modifier
+    oracle = newOracle;
+```
+
+**Proof of Concept**
+1. Attacker deposits 1 wei of wstETH via `CollateralVault.deposit` (needed only so
+   `collateralValueUsd`'s `amount == 0` guard at `LendingMarket.sol:136` is not hit).
+2. Attacker deploys `FakeOracle` whose `getPrice(wstETH)` returns `1e40` and whose
+   `getPrice(USDC)` returns `1e18`.
+3. Attacker calls `setOracle(FakeOracle)`.
+4. `collateralValueUsd(attacker)` is now astronomically large, `healthFactor` returns a huge value,
+   and `borrow(marketUsdcBalance)` passes the check at `LendingMarket.sol:203`.
+5. Attacker walks away with the treasury's entire USDC float and never repays.
+
+Alternatively the attacker inverts it — return a near-zero collateral price — and liquidates every
+borrower for the 5% bonus while their positions are genuinely healthy.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Add `onlyOwner`. Because an oracle swap is instantly fatal, also route it through the same timelock
+as C-1 so the change is observable before it takes effect:
+
+```solidity
+function setOracle(PriceOracle newOracle) external onlyOwner {
+```
+
