+## [H-3] `PriceOracle` accepts stale, negative and zero prices
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: evm-audit-oracles
+**Location**: `src/PriceOracle.sol:39-45` — `getPrice(address)`
+
+**Description**
+The oracle uses the deprecated `latestAnswer()` and performs no validation whatsoever:
+
+```solidity
+int256 answer = feed.latestAnswer();
+return uint256(answer) * 10 ** (18 - feed.decimals());
+```
+
+Four separate problems:
+
+1. **No staleness check.** `latestAnswer()` does not return `updatedAt`, so there is no way to know
+   how old the answer is. A feed that stopped updating hours ago is consumed as current.
+2. **No round-completeness check.** `answeredInRound`, `roundId` and `startedAt` are never
+   inspected. The `IAggregatorV3` interface already declares `latestRoundData()` — it is simply not
+   used.
+3. **Negative answers are cast to enormous positive numbers.** `uint256(int256(-1))` is
+   `2**256 - 1`. Chainlink feeds can and do report negative values during malfunction.
+4. **Zero is accepted.** A `0` price on the collateral feed makes every position instantly
+   liquidatable; a `0` price on the USDC feed makes `debtUsd == 0`, so `healthFactor` returns
+   `type(uint256).max` (`LendingMarket.sol:148`) and **every position becomes unliquidatable while
+   allowing unlimited borrowing**.
+
+There is also no `minAnswer`/`maxAnswer` circuit-breaker awareness: Chainlink aggregators clamp their
+reported value to those bounds, so in a genuine flash crash the feed keeps reporting `minAnswer`
+while the real price is far lower — the protocol accrues bad debt believing collateral is fine.
+
+**Proof of Concept**
+Case 4 is the sharpest. If the USDC/USD feed returns `0` — a stalled aggregator or a malfunction
+reporting zero — then for every user `debtValueUsd(user) = debtOf(user) * 0 / 1e6 = 0`, so
+`healthFactor` short-circuits to `type(uint256).max`. An attacker deposits 1 wei of wstETH, and
+`_borrow`'s health check at `LendingMarket.sol:203` passes for any amount. They borrow the entire
+USDC balance of the market against 1 wei of collateral. Simultaneously, `liquidate` reverts with
+`Healthy()` for every genuinely underwater position, so nothing can be unwound.
+
+Case 3: a negative wstETH answer makes `collateralValueUsd` overflow-large, with the same
+unlimited-borrow outcome.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Switch to `latestRoundData()` and validate everything, with a per-feed heartbeat rather than one
+global constant (Arbitrum feeds have different heartbeats — the USDC/USD and stETH/USD feeds do not
+share one):
+
+```solidity
+struct FeedConfig { IAggregatorV3 feed; uint32 heartbeat; uint8 decimals; }
+mapping(address => FeedConfig) public feeds;
+
+function getPrice(address token) external view returns (uint256) {
+    FeedConfig memory cfg = feeds[token];
+    if (address(cfg.feed) == address(0)) revert FeedNotSet(token);
+
+    (uint80 roundId, int256 answer, uint256 startedAt, uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound)
+        = cfg.feed.latestRoundData();
+
+    if (answer <= 0) revert InvalidPrice(token, answer);
+    if (startedAt == 0) revert RoundNotComplete(token);
+    if (answeredInRound < roundId) revert StaleRound(token);
+    if (block.timestamp - updatedAt > cfg.heartbeat) revert StalePrice(token, updatedAt);
+
+    return uint256(answer) * 10 ** (18 - cfg.decimals);
+}
+```
+
+Cache `decimals` at `setFeed` time rather than calling it on every price read (it is immutable per
+aggregator, and this also removes an external call from the hot loop in `collateralValueUsd`).
+Consider recording each feed's `minAnswer`/`maxAnswer` and reverting when the answer pins to either
+bound, so a clamped flash-crash price halts the market instead of silently accruing bad debt.
+
