+## [H-7] `liquidateAll` is an unbounded loop over an array anyone can stuff
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: evm-audit-dos
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:190-195` — `liquidateAll()`; root cause at `src/LendingMarket.sol:200` — `_borrow`
+
+**Description**
+`liquidateAll` iterates the entire `borrowers` array, calling `healthFactor` on each entry — which
+itself loops over every listed collateral token making external oracle and `decimals()` calls
+(`LendingMarket.sol:132-139`). The loop is `O(borrowers × collateralTokens)` external calls with no
+pagination and no bound.
+
+The array is attacker-growable, for free, from a single address. `_borrow` pushes on every call where
+the borrower's principal is currently zero:
+
+```solidity
+if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+Call `borrow(0)`: `principalOf` is `0`, so the address is pushed. `principalOf += 0` leaves it at
+`0`. `healthFactor` returns `type(uint256).max` because `debtUsd == 0`, so the check on line 203
+passes. `debtAsset.transfer(borrower, 0)` succeeds — USDC permits zero-value transfers. The
+transaction succeeds and the array is one entry longer. **The same address can do this unlimited
+times.**
+
+**Proof of Concept**
+1. Attacker calls `LendingMarket.borrow(0)` in a loop. Each iteration costs roughly one `SSTORE` for
+   the array push plus the array-length update — on the order of 25k gas. On Arbitrum that is a
+   fraction of a cent per push.
+2. A few hundred dollars of gas adds hundreds of thousands of entries.
+3. `liquidateAll()` now exceeds the block gas limit and reverts. It can never succeed again.
+4. There is no function anywhere in the codebase that removes an entry from `borrowers` — not
+   `repay`, not `_liquidate` (which zeroes `principalOf` on line 214 but leaves the array entry), not
+   an admin function. The array can only ever grow. **The keeper path is permanently and
+   irreversibly bricked**, and the only recovery is a proxy upgrade.
+
+The stated purpose of `liquidateAll` — "Run by the keeper after a large price move" — means this DoS
+lands precisely when the protocol most needs it, converting a griefing attack into protocol
+insolvency.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Three changes, all needed:
+
+1. **Reject zero-amount borrows** and only push genuinely new borrowers. Track membership with a
+   mapping rather than inferring it from `principalOf`:
+
+```solidity
+mapping(address => bool) public isBorrower;   // append to storage
+
+function _borrow(address borrower, uint256 amount) internal {
+    if (amount == 0) revert ZeroAmount();
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (!isBorrower[borrower]) { isBorrower[borrower] = true; borrowers.push(borrower); }
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+2. **Paginate.** Replace `liquidateAll()` with `liquidateMany(address[] calldata users)` and let the
+   keeper pass the specific underwater addresses it identified off-chain. This removes the on-chain
+   loop entirely and is strictly better than a `(start, end)` variant.
+
+3. **Remove entries on exit** (see M-6), using swap-and-pop with an index mapping, when a position is
+   fully repaid or liquidated.
+
+Note that even with pagination, `liquidateAll` reverting wholesale if *any* single `_liquidate`
+reverts (insufficient keeper allowance, a blacklisted address, one bad feed) remains a problem —
+`liquidateMany` should skip failures rather than revert the batch.
+
