+## [M-5] Implementation contract is left uninitialised
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: evm-audit-proxies
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:56-82` — `initialize(...)`
+
+**Description**
+`initialize` is `external` and guarded only by the `initialized` storage flag. That flag lives in the
+*proxy's* storage when called through `MarketProxy`, so the implementation contract deployed at its
+own address is left permanently uninitialised. Anyone can call `initialize` directly on the
+implementation and become its `owner`.
+
+There is no `_disableInitializers()` in a constructor, which is the standard mitigation.
+
+**Impact assessment, honestly stated**: the classic escalation from here is `delegatecall` to a
+`selfdestruct`, which bricks the proxy. That path is closed — `SELFDESTRUCT` no longer removes code
+post-Cancun, and there is no `delegatecall` in `LendingMarket` anyway. An attacker who owns the
+implementation can call `upgradeTo` on it, but that writes to the *implementation's* own
+`IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT`, which nothing reads. So the direct impact today is limited to a confusing
+on-chain state and an `Initialized`/`Upgraded` event trail on the implementation address that will
+alarm anyone monitoring it.
+
+It is listed at Medium rather than Low because it is free to fix, because it becomes materially
+dangerous the moment any future implementation contains a `delegatecall`, and because leaving it
+open makes the C-1 exploit harder to distinguish from noise during incident response.
+
+**Recommendation**
+```solidity
+constructor() {
+    initialized = true;   // locks the implementation; harmless in proxy context
+}
+```
+
+Note the constructor body runs at implementation-deployment time and writes to the implementation's
+own storage, so it does not affect the proxy. If you migrate to OpenZeppelin's upgradeable base,
+use `_disableInitializers()` instead.
+
+---
+
+## [M-6] `borrowers` accumulates duplicates and never shrinks
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: evm-audit-dos, evm-audit-general
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:200` (`_borrow`), `src/LendingMarket.sol:214` (`_liquidate`)
+
+**Description**
+Separate from the deliberate stuffing attack in H-7, the array degrades under entirely normal usage:
+
+- `_borrow` pushes whenever `principalOf[borrower] == 0`. A user who borrows, fully repays, and
+  borrows again is pushed a second time. Every repay/re-borrow cycle adds an entry.
+- `_liquidate` sets `principalOf[user] = 0` but leaves the array entry in place — so a liquidated
+  user who returns is pushed again too.
+- Nothing ever removes an entry. There is no removal code anywhere in the codebase.
+
+Every duplicate and every stale entry costs a full `healthFactor` evaluation — which is itself a loop
+over all collateral tokens with external oracle calls — on every `liquidateAll()`. A normally
+successful protocol degrades its own keeper path over time.
+
+Note also that L-1 guarantees `principalOf` almost never actually reaches zero after a repay (1 wei
+of dust remains), which suppresses the duplicate-push in practice but replaces it with a different
+problem: those users stay in the array forever with permanently non-zero dust debt.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Track membership explicitly and remove on exit with swap-and-pop:
+
+```solidity
+mapping(address => uint256) public borrowerIndex;   // 1-based; 0 == not present
+
+function _addBorrower(address b) internal {
+    if (borrowerIndex[b] != 0) return;
+    borrowers.push(b);
+    borrowerIndex[b] = borrowers.length;
+}
+
+function _removeBorrower(address b) internal {
+    uint256 idx = borrowerIndex[b];
+    if (idx == 0) return;
+    address last = borrowers[borrowers.length - 1];
+    borrowers[idx - 1] = last;
+    borrowerIndex[last] = idx;
+    borrowers.pop();
+    delete borrowerIndex[b];
+}
+```
+
+Call `_removeBorrower` when `principalOf` reaches zero in `repay` and in `_liquidate`. Fixing L-1 is
+a prerequisite for this to actually trigger on repay.
+
+---
+
+## [M-7] USDC debt is priced by oracle, so a depeg loosens every position
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: evm-audit-oracles, evm-audit-defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:141-143` — `debtValueUsd(address)`
+
+**Description**
+```solidity
+return debtOf(user) * oracle.getPrice(address(debtAsset)) / 10 ** debtAsset.decimals();
+```
+
+Debt is denominated in USDC and repaid in USDC, but its value for solvency purposes is marked to the
+USDC/USD feed. When USDC trades below $1, every borrower's debt shrinks in the health calculation
+even though the number of USDC they owe has not changed.
+
+USDC traded at $0.87 during the March 2023 SVB weekend. At that price, `debtValueUsd` reports 13%
+less than the borrower actually owes, and every position gains roughly 15% of borrowing headroom.
+
+**Proof of Concept**
+1. USDC depegs to $0.87.
+2. Alice has $10,000 of collateral and 8,000 USDC of debt. `debtValueUsd` now reports $6,960 instead
+   of $8,000.
+3. Alice borrows another ~1,190 USDC against the same collateral, at a moment when the protocol's
+   own risk parameters would have said she was at her limit.
+4. USDC repegs to $1.00. Alice is now underwater by the protocol's rules, through a mechanism the
+   protocol itself enabled. If the collateral has also moved, this is bad debt (H-9).
+
+The reverse case is worse for users: a USDC price *above* $1 inflates every debt and mass-liquidates
+borrowers who did nothing.
+
+**Recommendation**
+There is a legitimate design argument for marking the debt asset to market — it is not simply wrong.
+But it needs bounds. Clamp the debt-asset price to a narrow band around the peg and halt borrowing
+outside it:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 price = oracle.getPrice(address(debtAsset));
+if (price < 0.98e18 || price > 1.02e18) revert DebtAssetDepegged(price);
+```
+
+Halting new borrows (but continuing to allow repay and liquidation) during a depeg is the
+conservative choice and avoids both directions of the problem. Decide deliberately and document it —
+right now the behaviour looks incidental rather than chosen.
+
+---
+
+## [M-8] Unbounded `borrowRate`, no pause, no timelock, immutable owner
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: evm-audit-access-control
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:101-105` (`setBorrowRate`), contract-wide
+
+**Description**
+Assuming C-1/C-2/C-3 are fixed and all setters become `onlyOwner`, the remaining admin surface is
+still concentrated:
+
+- **`setBorrowRate` has no upper bound.** The owner can set 1,000,000 bps. `accrueInterest` will not
+  overflow at that magnitude, so it simply makes every borrower instantly insolvent and liquidatable
+  at the next accrual. The function correctly calls `accrueInterest()` first, so past interest is not
+  retroactively repriced — that part is right.
+- **`owner` can never be changed.** `LendingMarket` has no `transferOwnership`, no two-step handover,
+  and no renounce. The address passed to `initialize` at deployment is the owner forever. A lost key
+  means collateral can never be listed or delisted and the rate can never be changed again. A
+  compromised key can never be rotated.
+- **No pause.** There is no way to halt borrowing or liquidation during an incident — an oracle
+  malfunction, a depeg, a discovered bug. The only lever is a proxy upgrade.
+- **No timelock on anything.** `setLiquidationThreshold` in particular takes effect instantly and can
+  make thousands of positions liquidatable in the same block it is called, with no window for
+  borrowers to react.
+
+**Recommendation**
+- Bound `borrowRate` (e.g. `<= 10_000` bps) and emit the old and new values.
+- Add two-step ownership transfer (`Ownable2Step`) to `LendingMarket`.
+- Add a `Pausable` guard on `borrow`, `borrowWithSig` and `CollateralVault.deposit`. Deliberately do
+  **not** pause `repay`, `withdraw` or `liquidate`: pausing repay traps borrowers who are being
+  liquidated, and pausing liquidation converts a price move into a solvency crisis.
+- Put `upgradeTo`, `setOracle` and `setLiquidationThreshold` behind a timelock with the owner as a
+  multisig. These three are the ones where instant execution is indistinguishable from a rug.
+
