+## [L-8] ETH sent to the proxy is unrecoverable
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: evm-audit-general
+**Location**: `src/MarketProxy.sol:38` — `receive()`
+
+**Description**
+`MarketProxy` declares `receive() external payable {}` and a `payable` fallback, so it accepts ETH.
+`LendingMarket` has no `payable` function and no mechanism to move ETH, so anything sent is stuck
+permanently. The proxy can also be force-fed via `selfdestruct` regardless, but the explicit
+`receive()` invites accidental sends.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Remove `receive()` — the protocol has no use for ETH. If you keep it, add an owner-only ETH sweep to
+the implementation.
+
+---
+
+## [L-9] `MarketProxy` constructor delegatecalls without a code check
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: evm-audit-proxies, evm-audit-assembly
+**Location**: `src/MarketProxy.sol:10-24` — `constructor`
+
+**Description**
+```solidity
+sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, implementation)
+...
+(bool ok, bytes memory ret) = implementation.delegatecall(initData);
+```
+
+Neither the slot write nor the `delegatecall` checks that `implementation` has code. A `delegatecall`
+to a codeless address returns `success = true`, so deploying the proxy with a wrong or not-yet-mined
+implementation address succeeds silently, emits `Upgraded`, and produces a proxy where every call
+returns empty success. Same root cause as the secondary failure mode in C-1.
+
+**Recommendation**
+```solidity
+require(implementation.code.length > 0, "impl has no code");
+```
+
+Also note the revert-bubbling assembly on lines 19-22 reads `mload(ret)` after `ret` may be
+zero-length; it works, but returning a zero-length revert reason on failure loses the underlying
+error. Bubbling with a fallback message would make deployment failures diagnosable.
+
+---
+
+## [L-10] Liquidators have no slippage protection
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: evm-audit-defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:183-187` — `liquidate(address)`
+
+**Description**
+`liquidate(address user)` takes no `minCollateralOut` and no `maxRepayAmount`. Between a liquidator
+submitting and the transaction executing, `accrueInterest()` can increase `debt` (raising what they
+pay) and an oracle update can change the seize price (lowering what they receive). The liquidator has
+no way to bound either.
+
+**Recommendation**
+```solidity
+function liquidate(address user, uint256 maxRepay, uint256 minSeizeUsd) external
+```
+
+Revert if the actual repay exceeds `maxRepay` or the seized value falls below `minSeizeUsd`. This
+matters more once H-9's partial liquidation lands, since the liquidator will be choosing a repay size.
+
+---
+
+## [L-11] Self-liquidation is permitted
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: evm-audit-defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:183-187` — `liquidate(address)`
+
+**Description**
+Nothing prevents `msg.sender == user`. A borrower can liquidate their own position, repaying their
+debt and seizing their own collateral plus the 5% bonus — which comes out of their own remaining
+collateral, so it is close to value-neutral in the healthy case.
+
+It becomes an edge case worth caring about with bad debt (H-9): a borrower whose collateral is worth
+less than `debt * 1.05` can self-liquidate to cap their loss at the collateral value while the
+protocol absorbs the shortfall, choosing the moment deliberately rather than waiting for a third
+party.
+
+**Recommendation**
+Low priority, and there is a reasonable argument for allowing self-liquidation as a clean exit. Decide
+deliberately and document it. If you disallow it, `if (msg.sender == user) revert SelfLiquidation();`
+is sufficient.
+
+---
+
+# Informational
+
+## [I-1] No test suite
+
+**Severity**: Info
+**Category**: process
+**Location**: repo root; README "No dependencies and no test suite yet — the suite lands with the audit fixes."
+
+Shipping a lending market to mainnet in two weeks with zero tests is the single largest process risk
+here, and it compounds every finding above: none of the recommended fixes can be regression-checked,
+and C-4 in particular is the kind of bug a single arithmetic assertion would have caught immediately.
+
+Minimum before deploy: unit tests for `healthFactor` across the full ratio range (this catches C-4),
+invariant/fuzz tests for `borrow → accrue → repay` round-tripping to zero (L-1) and for
+`sum(vault.balanceOf) == token.balanceOf(vault)` (M-9, H-2), fork tests against real Arbitrum USDC and
+wstETH with the real Chainlink feeds (H-3, H-5, H-6), and access-control tests asserting every
+external setter reverts for a non-owner (C-1, C-2, C-3 — one parameterised test would have caught all
+three).
+
+## [I-2] `evm_version` is not pinned
+
+**Severity**: Info
+**Category**: evm-audit-chain-specific
+**Location**: `foundry.toml`
+
+`foundry.toml` sets `solc = "0.8.28"` but does not set `evm_version`, so the build relies on the solc
+default matching what ArbOS supports. Arbitrum One supports Cancun opcodes on current ArbOS, so this
+is fine today — but it is an implicit dependency on toolchain defaults for a contract that handles
+real money. Pin it explicitly:
+
+```toml
+evm_version = "cancun"   # confirm against the ArbOS version live on Arbitrum One at deploy time
+```
+
+## [I-3] `Liquidated` does not log what was seized
+
+**Severity**: Info
+**Category**: evm-audit-general
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:49, 233` — `_liquidate`
+
+`event Liquidated(address indexed borrower, address indexed liquidator, uint256 debtRepaid)` records
+the debt but not which collateral tokens were seized or in what amounts. The seize loop can touch
+several tokens. `CollateralVault.Seized` covers it partially, but reconstructing a liquidation
+requires correlating across two contracts. For a protocol with no test suite and no indexer yet, event
+completeness is what incident response will depend on.
+
+Also missing: `_liquidate` emits nothing when the seize loop exits with `seizeUsd > 0` — the bad-debt
+case in H-9 leaves no on-chain trace at all.
+
